   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to removing the bonding\nrequirement for independent adjusters",
    "basePrintNo": "S5761",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5761-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2108, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5761\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to removing the bonding\nrequirement for independent adjusters\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo modernize the insurance law to remove an anachronistic provision\nrelating to a mandate upon independent adjusters\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill removes independent adjusters from the require-\nment that a bond be procured by a licensee and filed with the super-\nintendent of financial services.\n\nSection two provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSection 2108 provides a range of requirements upon licensees holding\nindependent insurance adjuster licenses, among other licenses issued by\nthe Department of Financial Services. The requirement which is the\nsubject of this legislation requires prospective or renewing licensees\nto post a bond issued by a surety in the amount of $ 1,000.00 with the\nsuperintendent. It ostensibly is to assure the \"faithful performance\" of\nthe licensees. This provision, like others, harken to a day when\nenforcement was a vastly different task for the regulator. However, with\nthe onset of the digital age, among other advances, the need to have a\nbond \"at risk\" is no longer a major impetus to compelling compliant\nbehavior by licensees.\n\nThe bond requirement also has created a filing challenge for both the\nlicensee and the regulator. Delays have been occasioned when the bond\nand the license have not been co-terminously provided to and approved by\nthe regulator simply because there are several other people necessarily\nin the process. Removing this filing requirement will streamline the\nfiling process without sacrificing any meaningful enforcement tool and\nremove a cost of doing business as an independent adjuster in the State.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: A.10592-Remained in Insurance.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n",
